WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.390
Choosing the perfect actor for a movie role
is already a delicate process, but it becomes

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.410
even more difficult when an actor leaves a
production, which can happen for any number

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.760
of reasons.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.059
Directors and casting professionals can be
left scrambling to replace their departing

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.059
stars.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.949
Here are some of the craziest last-minute
replacements we've seen on the big screen

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:21.590
— some of which ended up being the replacement
actor's most successful role.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.260
Mike Myers in Shrek

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.080
It's hard to imagine anything but Mike Myers'
voice coming from the lovable animated ogre,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.990
but the story of Shrek's casting is actually
quite tragic.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:35.450
Originally, comedian and character actor Chris
Farley had been cast in the role, and according

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.559
to his brother Kevin, Farley actually completed
recording nearly all of his dialogue for the

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:40.699
original Shrek film.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.520
"People see me and they go, 'AHHH!

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.440
Help!

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.960
A big, stupid, stinky, smelly, ugly ogre!"

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.479
Unfortunately, Farley had been struggling
for some time with alcohol and substance abuse,

00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:57.280
and in December 1997, he passed away in his
Chicago apartment after an accidental drug

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.280
overdose.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.129
DreamWorks rushed to find a replacement, and
chose Myers.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:05.670
Additionally, they recast the role of Fiona
with Cameron Diaz, who replaced Farley's S.N.L.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.729
castmate Janeane Garofalo.

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:09.939
Christian Bale in American Psycho

00:01:09.939 --> 00:01:14.090
In an odd turn of events, Christian Bale ended
up replacing himself for the role of Patrick

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.369
Bateman in the 2000 black comedy horror film
American Psycho — well, sort of.

00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:22.969
Director Mary Harron had chosen Bale for the
role, but the British actor wasn't yet the

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:24.659
superstar he is today.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:29.409
Executives at Lionsgate wanted a higher-profile
actor, and reportedly made a $20 million offer

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:32.200
to Leonardo DiCaprio — without informing
Harron.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.979
Harron fought hard to keep Bale, but Lionsgate
eventually removed her from the project, too,

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:38.869
replacing her with Oliver Stone.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:42.999
Stone began revising the script, and the film
fell into a period of development hell.

00:01:42.999 --> 00:01:46.909
Eventually, DiCaprio left to film The Beach,
and Stone walked out as well.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:50.890
When Lionsgate finally came back to Harron,
she agreed to return as director, with Bale

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.189
as Bateman.

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:57.119
Their perseverance definitely paid off, as
the film became a polarizing box office success

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:01.340
and eventual cult classic — helping propel
Bale to the top of Hollywood's A-list.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:08.030
"Well, isn't this a coincidence?"

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.929
Hugh Jackman in X-Men

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.920
Although he ended up landing the role of Wolverine
in X-Men, Hugh Jackman wasn't director Bryan

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.470
Singer's first choice.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.580
Originally, Singer wanted Russell Crowe, who
turned it down.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.690
Crowe recommended his friend, Jackman, for
the part, but Singer decided to go with the

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.140
more experienced actor Dougray Scott.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.819
Unfortunately for Scott, production on Mission:
Impossible 2 was delayed, leaving him unavailable

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:30.610
when filming for X-Men began.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.240
Forced to make a last-minute change in the
main cast, Singer called in Jackman for an

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.200
audition, gave him the role, and the rest
is history.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.299
Geena Davis in A League of Their Own

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:43.730
With her performance as Dottie Hinson in the
1992 sports dramedy A League of Their Own,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.620
Geena Davis won the hearts of moviegoers everywhere.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.689
"Hey, who's the goddamned manager here, I
AM!"

00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:50.870
"Then act like it, ya big lush!"

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.040
However, director Penny Marshall's original
choice was actress Debra Winger, who was furious

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.989
when she learned that Marshall had cast pop
superstar Madonna in the film.

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:02.939
Marshall later recalled in her memoir that
before walking off the set, Winger told her,

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:04.970
"You're making an Elvis film!"

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.629
Subsequently, Davis nailed her audition in
Marshall's backyard — and proved that she

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:09.629
could throw a ball.

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:12.819
While the rest of the cast had been training
together for months prior to filming, Davis

00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:16.680
had to work hard and fast to get into shape
for the demanding role.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.349
Michael Biehn in Aliens

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.659
It's probably hard to picture anyone but Michael
Biehn as Dwayne Hicks in the 1986 movie Aliens,

00:03:23.659 --> 00:03:26.079
but originally, it was supposed to be James
Remar.

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:30.349
However, a couple of weeks after filming started,
Remar abruptly left the production.

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:34.409
He later revealed during a Sidebar podcast
interview that while he had originally cited

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:38.549
"urgent matters at home" to explain his departure,
the truth is that he was struggling with drug

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.660
addiction at the time, and director James
Cameron fired Remar after he was arrested

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:43.750
for possession.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.239
With no time to prepare for the physically
demanding role, Michael Biehn was brought

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:49.200
in as Remar's replacement.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.049
Ed Harris in The Truman Show

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:54.590
The critically acclaimed satirical film The
Truman Show wouldn't be the same without the

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.000
character Christof, the obsessive showrunner
played masterfully by Ed Harris.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.370
But director Peter Weir didn't choose Harris
at first — originally, Dennis Hopper was

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:04.659
cast in the role.

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:09.280
After filming began, Weir and Hopper reportedly
ran into creative differences on the set,

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.480
leading Hopper to leave the production entirely.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:15.549
Hopper later revealed that he was fired after
only two days of filming — a decision he

00:04:15.549 --> 00:04:17.660
called "a major blow" at the time.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.750
Called up at the last minute to replace Hopper,
Harris threw himself into the role — earning

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:26.500
himself an Oscar nomination and a Golden Globe
win for Best Supporting Actor.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.440
Clint Eastwood in Dirty Harry

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.820
In addition to his performance as the Man
With No Name, Clint Eastwood is also frequently

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.750
remembered for his portrayal of the hard-as-nails
homicide cop Harry Callahan in Dirty Harry.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.090
But the role almost went to another famous
performer: Frank Sinatra.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.560
Supposedly, the script written for Sinatra
had Harry toting a 12-gauge shotgun as his

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.810
signature weapon.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:52.040
But as production got underway, Sinatra had
to back out — a broken hand he sustained

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.790
while filming a fight scene for The Manchurian
Candidate left him unable to hold a gun.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.000
After ensuring the original script would be
used, Eastwood agreed to play Harry — cementing

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.380
his place in pop-culture history with a 44
Magnum and the frequently misquoted line,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:11.180
"You've got to ask yourself one question:
'Do I feel lucky?'

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.920
Well, do you, punk?"

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.920
Thanks for watching!

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.320
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.650
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

